<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Megara Hyblaea</h1><p class="st-meta">728 BCE – 483 BCE (approx.) · capital Megara Hyblaea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek colony on Sicily's east coast whose excavated remains are a key source for early Greek town planning, destroyed by Gelon of Syracuse in 483 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/megara">Megara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, after failed attempts elsewhere, were offered land by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicels">Sicel</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyblon">Hyblon</a> and founded Megara Hyblaea around 728 BCE, on the coast north of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>. The name honoured both their mother city and their native host.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Excavations by French archaeologists have shown that the town was laid out with regular streets and house plots soon after its foundation, among the earliest planned Greek settlements known. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> with temples and stoas developed in the 7th century BCE. Its territory was limited by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leontini">Leontini</a>, so around 628 BCE it founded the more distant colony of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/selinous">Selinous</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon of Syracuse</a> captured Megara Hyblaea in 483 BCE, moved its wealthy citizens to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a> and sold the common people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. A smaller town was refounded in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> in the 340s BCE and destroyed again by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 214 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/megara-hyblaea.jpg" alt="Megara Hyblaea" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Clemensfranz · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Megara_Hyblaea_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>728 BCE – 483 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Megara Hyblaea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-606&amp;focus=megara-hyblaea" aria-label="Megara Hyblaea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5060 838 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Megara Hyblaea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5380 1094L5383 1099"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 1099h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 1094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 1094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-606&amp;focus=megara-hyblaea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

